// The Road to Tataka — Bala Kanda
// Source: Valmiki, Bala Kanda, sargas 22-25 (verify)
// Staged for the painted scene: a watercolour dawn; the mantras travel as points of gold.
// The archery practice is the game's own addition.

>> look dawn
>> scene riverbank
>> cast vishvamitra at 0.7 face=left
>> cast rama:prince at 0.44
>> cast lakshmana:prince at 0.3
The south bank of the Sarayu, at first light. The sage stops where the water runs clear.
>> pose vishvamitra raise
VISHVAMITRA: Take water in your hands, Rama. I will give you two mantras, Bala and Atibala.
>> together
>> pose rama kneel
>> pose lakshmana kneel
>> wait 0.8
>> mantra vishvamitra rama,lakshmana
>> sound bell
VISHVAMITRA: With them you will not tire, or hunger, or be caught asleep.
>> pose vishvamitra stand
>> together
>> pose rama stand
>> pose lakshmana stand
>> wait 0.6
They sleep that night on grass by the river. In the morning a boat takes them over the Ganga.
>> pose vishvamitra point
VISHVAMITRA: Before we go further, show me your arm. Three marks. Hit them.
>> battle sarayuTargets
>> pose vishvamitra namaste
VISHVAMITRA: Good. A full draw goes through more than one thing. Remember that.
>> scene waste clear
>> look dusk
>> music grief
>> together
>> enter vishvamitra from left to 0.62 in 3.4
>> enter rama:prince from left to 0.44 in 3
>> enter lakshmana:prince from left to 0.3 in 2.8
Ahead the forest turns grey. No bird calls.
>> face lakshmana right
LAKSHMANA: There were villages here. Where are the people?
>> face vishvamitra left
VISHVAMITRA: Gone. A yakshini named Tataka took this country. She has the strength of a thousand elephants, and she eats what she catches.
>> pose rama hold
RAMA: Must I kill a woman, master?
>> pose vishvamitra point
VISHVAMITRA: A prince protects his people, whatever shape the danger takes. That is the whole of it.
>> pose vishvamitra stand
>> pose rama namaste
RAMA: Then I will do as you say.
-> END
